WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.720
Hi everyone, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 54 for May 29th, 2026.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:11.400
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:16.160
Some awesome AI developments today and we're going to make all of it make sense.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:22.080
Let's get into it. So imagine texting a friend who actually remembers every single thing you said

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:28.680
five minutes ago and responds in the same casual tone. That is exactly what Sesame just brought to

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:34.180
your iPhone with their new free app. Instead of typing stiff commands and getting robotic replies,

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.760
you speak naturally and the AI keeps the whole conversation flowing like a real back-and-forth

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:45.040
chat. Think of it like the difference between sending a formal email and leaving quick voice notes

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:50.720
with someone who listens and stays on topic. The AI holds on to a short conversation history,

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:56.540
the same way you remember the last few messages in a group chat. So it does not reset after every

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:01.700
single line. This matters because most of us already live on our phones chatting with friends or

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:08.520
scrolling feeds. An AI that joins that same natural flow could help explain homework, brainstorm story

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:15.080
ideas, or practice a new language without feeling awkward. For students, thinking about future careers,

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:21.220
it shows AI moving from a search engine that talks towards something more like a helpful companion you

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:26.800
carry everywhere. You can download the Sesame app straight from the app store and start talking right

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:33.820
away. Try asking it to explain a school topic in a fun way or brainstorm a creative project and notice

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:39.940
how the replies stay on track even when you switch subjects. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show,

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:46.120
the deep dive, where we look under the hood. You know how your phone sometimes guesses the next word

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:51.260
while you're texting. Now picture that same autocomplete remembering everything you have said in

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:56.640
the last few minutes and using that memory to build a whole helpful reply instead of just one word.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:03.620
The AI first listens to your voice or text and breaks it into small chunks it understands. It then

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:08.720
checks the conversation history it has been keeping like flipping back through recent messages in

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:14.660
a group chat. Finally, it puts together an answer that feels connected to what came before. Instead of

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:20.100
treating every message like a brand new question. When the system gets good at these three steps,

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:25.160
replies stop feeling like random guesses and start feeling like talking with someone who knows the

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:32.040
whole story so far. And that is basically how a conversational AI agent holds a natural back and forth

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:39.360
instead of resetting after every line. Not so scary, right? Next up, something cool you can try right

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:45.800
now if you like design or school projects. Adobe built a conversational AI helper that lives inside

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:51.440
its design tools and takes simple, spoken or typed instructions to create or tweak images.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:57.920
It keeps the conversation going so you can say make the background warmer or add more space on the

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:03.880
left and it remembers what you already made. Think of it like having a patient intern sitting next to you

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:10.560
who never gets tired of small changes. Go to Adobe's Firefly website or open the tool inside Photoshop,

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:16.420
type a short description of something you want to design, then follow up with one change at a time.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:22.260
You may need a free Adobe account to try the full chat version, but the experiment is free to start.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:29.440
Here is another one that turns your iPhone into a private AI playground. A new guide shows how to

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:35.460
install a small AI model that runs completely offline on your phone so nothing leaves the device.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:41.700
It is like keeping a private notebook that never sends pages to the cloud. This keeps conversations

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:47.840
private and works even when you have no signal. Open the Engadget article on your phone,

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:53.540
follow the simple steps using free apps already in the app store and within minutes you can ask the

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:59.820
local chatbot to write a short story or quiz you on vocabulary. One more quick experiment that shows

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:06.340
how far a regular chat can take you. A Reddit user used Claude to plan an entire apartment renovation

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:11.940
from choosing styles to calculating exact measurements so contractors got clear instructions.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:18.600
The AI helped keep colors and shapes consistent across rooms like having a second set of eyes that

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:26.080
remembers every detail you mentioned. Openclaw.ai on your phone or laptop describe a space you want to

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:32.640
improve, even if it is just your desk setup and ask for layout or color suggestions while you reply

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:40.000
with what you like or do not like. In QuickBits, scientists built a robot shape like a sea urchin that uses

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:46.780
20 flexible legs to grip walls and move through trees. The design could help future robots explore places

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:54.020
that wheels or normal legs cannot reach. Meanwhile, Waymo is bringing a new pale blue self-driving car

00:04:54.020 --> 00:05:00.580
called Ohai to roads in California and Arizona. Regular passengers will soon ride in vehicles that

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:06.360
navigate cities without a human driver behind the wheel. And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:12.800
made you go, huh, that's cool. Go play with it. Curiosity is how every expert started. See you tomorrow,

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:19.360
and if you'd rather watch than listen, find us on YouTube at at Nerra Network. Links in the show notes.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:26.320
This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice. Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
